App install scripts
===================

Each file apps/<name>.sh installs one third-party application that is not a
plain apt package from the base distribution (it needs its own apt repository,
a downloaded .deb, a tarball, ...). Plain distro packages belong in
packages/*.list instead.

`ryad apps` lists the apps; `ryad apps add|rm <name>` picks them (it edits
APPS in ~/.config/ryad/build.conf); `ryad apps new <name>` writes your own
installer in ~/.config/ryad/apps/, from a template. Your own apps are
installed unless APPS removes them (-name).
claude-desktop and claude-code are always installed, in their own layers.
Each other app gets its own layer, in the order of APPS.

Contract
--------
- Runs as root during `podman build`, with bash, from any directory.
- Starts with `. "$(dirname "$0")/lib.sh"` for set -euo pipefail, $ARCH,
  $CODENAME, $ID, log/die, apt_clean and set_default_handler.
- Must work on any Debian/Ubuntu base (read /etc/os-release, never hard-code
  a codename).
- Installs system-wide (/opt, /usr/local, ...), never under /home: at runtime
  the home directory is the profile.
- Version: read ${<NAME>_VERSION} where <NAME> is the app name upper-cased,
  '-' and '.' replaced by '_' (e.g. RSTUDIO_VERSION). Treat empty or "latest"
  as "newest available". Any FOO_VERSION=... line in build.conf is passed to
  app scripts automatically.
- Ends with apt_clean if it used apt.

Header lines
------------
Comment lines of the form "# @key value" describe the app to ryad:

  # @description TEXT      one line, shown by `ryad apps`
  # @commands CMD...       the commands it adds (`ryad apps`, shell banner)
  # @needs APP...          apps it relies on (e.g. firefox, for sign-in
                           pages); ryad warns when one isn't installed
  # @latest COMMAND        prints the newest version, on one line. Run by
                           `ryad build` before building, in a throwaway
                           container of the previous image (it has curl and
                           jq), with BASE_ID, BASE_VERSION, BASE_CODENAME and
                           DEB_ARCH set for the image being built. The result
                           is passed as <NAME>_VERSION, so a new release
                           rebuilds the app's layer. Without it, "latest" is
                           only looked up again by `ryad build --refresh`.

Minimal example (apps/foo.sh):

    # @description Foo, a command-line agent
    # @commands foo
    . "$(dirname "$0")/lib.sh"
    curl -fsSL https://example.com/key.asc -o /usr/share/keyrings/foo.asc
    echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/foo.asc] https://example.com/apt stable main" \
      > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/foo.list
    apt-get update
    v=${FOO_VERSION:-latest}
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends "foo$([ "$v" = latest ] || echo "=$v")"
    apt_clean
